(3846107, 539571, 77192, '<td valign="top"><table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by SoulMan1978:</b></div><hr/><i> nice idea. I''m guessing somewhere like the CN Tower at night? I''ll have a look tomorrow hehe </i></td></tr></table> <br/> <br/>Sky tower? Aaaaaggghhhhhh! I don''t believe I didn''t get that!! I''ve been up there!!! :-(<br/> <br/>Well done once again on a great shot. Imaginatively done. :-)<br/><br/><i>Message edited by author 2007-06-28 18:30:50.</i></td>', 'Originally posted by SoulMan1978: nice idea. I''m guessing somewhere like the CN Tower at night? I''ll have a look tomorrow hehe   Sky tower? Aaaaaggghhhhhh! I don''t believe I didn''t get that!! I''ve been up there!!! :-( Well done once again on a great shot. Imaginatively done. :-)', '2007-06-28 19:29:57.000000', '2007-06-28 19:30:50.000000', 0),
(3846163, 539142, 5508, '<td valign="top">I don''t comment on winning photos. But I have to say that this is the best photo on DP Challenge. Probably because you captured the feeling of Star Gazing dead on. I should know because I do alot of it with my scope my self. Good Job.</td>', 'I don''t comment on winning photos. But I have to say that this is the best photo on DP Challenge. Probably because you captured the feeling of Star Gazing dead on. I should know because I do alot of it with my scope my self. Good Job.', '2007-06-28 19:51:04.000000', NULL, 0),
(3846182, 539559, 53548, '<td valign="top">i didnt see this in the voting, and i didnt really vote much, but i would have given this a 9-10. superb work . hats off</td>', 'i didnt see this in the voting, and i didnt really vote much, but i would have given this a 9-10. superb work . hats off', '2007-06-28 19:57:15.000000', NULL, 0),
(3846230, 538205, 66398, '<td valign="top"><table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by eschelar:</b></div><hr/><i> I still feel that the negative space in this image is not a strong enough element to avoid a DNMC. No amount of processing changes this as this is a content issue. I think it is improved slightly in the two more severe crops, but it''s still a bit of a shoehorn. </i></td></tr></table> <br/> <br/>Thanks for the input and while I appreciate the attempt and the opinion. I still prefer my image over your edits. I like the image and apparently so did quite a few others on DPC since it did place 10th out of 299 entries. <br/> <br/>As you said in your email to me there were things that I did in editing that you couldn''t get the same so we each have our different combinations and ways of editing. One is not right over the other nor better or worse than the other. It all comes down to taste and what people like. You may not like some of the things about my shot and may think that my DPL team was wrong to have said I should enter this but if you take a look and compare my average and my highest challenge was before entering the DPL and look at them now then it is quite obvious that there has been a tremendous immprovement over that short amount of time. <br/> <br/>I don''t claim that my entry was the best it could be nor that it was the best in the challenge. Why you picked out my entry to comment on I don''t know but there were several shots in that challenge that had different takes on negative space and while commenting on every entry in that challenge I admitted that their take was different than mine but still good. There were also quite a few (many in fact) who had the same idea that I did.<br/> <br/>Anyway thanks for your input and while your edit has its merits I still prefer mine.<br/> <br/></td>', 'Originally posted by eschelar: I still feel that the negative space in this image is not a strong enough element to avoid a DNMC. No amount of processing changes this as this is a content issue. I think it is improved slightly in the two more severe crops, but it''s still a bit of a shoehorn.   Thanks for the input and while I appreciate the attempt and the opinion. I still prefer my image over your edits. I like the image and apparently so did quite a few others on DPC since it did place 10th out of 299 entries.  As you said in your email to me there were things that I did in editing that you couldn''t get the same so we each have our different combinations and ways of editing. One is not right over the other nor better or worse than the other. It all comes down to taste and what people like. You may not like some of the things about my shot and may think that my DPL team was wrong to have said I should enter this but if you take a look and compare my average and my highest challenge was before entering the DPL and look at them now then it is quite obvious that there has been a tremendous immprovement over that short amount of time.  I don''t claim that my entry was the best it could be nor that it was the best in the challenge. Why you picked out my entry to comment on I don''t know but there were several shots in that challenge that had different takes on negative space and while commenting on every entry in that challenge I admitted that their take was different than mine but still good. There were also quite a few (many in fact) who had the same idea that I did. Anyway thanks for your input and while your edit has its merits I still prefer mine. ', '2007-06-28 20:07:54.000000', NULL, 0),
(3846308, 539142, 11769, '<td valign="top">I''ve spent many a night under the stars the Moon my only companion. The night has a different smell the sounds are different in a way a different world than the day in light. This photograph with the expanse of stars overhead emphases the lonely human gazing up in wonder at the immensity of everything. You captured that feeling perfectly, with great skill. This is a photograph to be proud of.</td>', 'I''ve spent many a night under the stars the Moon my only companion. The night has a different smell the sounds are different in a way a different world than the day in light. This photograph with the expanse of stars overhead emphases the lonely human gazing up in wonder at the immensity of everything. You captured that feeling perfectly, with great skill. This is a photograph to be proud of.', '2007-06-28 20:42:24.000000', NULL, 0),
(3846380, 537066, 6519, '<td valign="top">LOL, no wonder I so often have a hard time deciding which photo to actually enter in a challenge - there simply is no pleasing everyone!<br/> <br/>Six people mentioned the lean/angle of this photo - three liked it, three didn''t.<br/> <br/>A good reminder to simply do what WE like, and hope that enough others agree  :-)</td>', 'LOL, no wonder I so often have a hard time deciding which photo to actually enter in a challenge - there simply is no pleasing everyone! Six people mentioned the lean/angle of this photo - three liked it, three didn''t. A good reminder to simply do what WE like, and hope that enough others agree  :-)', '2007-06-28 21:07:45.000000', NULL, 0),
(3846590, 538205, 28710, '<td valign="top">And that right there is probably why your DPL teammates suggested that you submit it for the challenge. For starters, they are more attuned to the public appeal of an image.<br/> <br/>It''s a good question as to why this image caught my attention. I found that the image really stood out to me among the top 30. (my habit is to view the top 30-50 and bottom 20 of each challenge)<br/> <br/>I just double checked the top 30 images. This is the only image that really stands out as having an issue where there IS negative space, but that negative space plays no significant role in the image.<br/> <br/>Yes, it''s the complementary color to the reds in the feathers of the hawk, but how is that doing anything of note in the image. It is simply a background. the impact of the negative space in this image would not change the image significantly by cropping most of it away. <br/> <br/>That cannot be said of any other image in the top 30. <br/> <br/>I''m not trying to say that I don''t like the image. In fact, I do like the image. that''s why I put the time into the alternate edits to try to give you some options from that really heavily processed look.<br/> <br/>What I don''t like about the image is that it looks so overprocessed. What I don''t like about it in this challenge is that it is DNMC.<br/>What I don''t like about that is not so much that you entered it (we are all entitled to our own takes on the challenge description) but that this is a strong negative effect of the DPL. The DPL has increased the number of ''shoehorn'' entries and this is a very plain demonstration of that. That the DPL is helping you individually is great. Shoot a pic, put it on your DPL forum and learn. Wonderful! I''m all for it.<br/> <br/>negative space does not = free study.<br/>And while we are there, DNMC does not = this is a bad pic.<br/> <br/>Perhaps you could mention something in the image details about how you feel the Negative Space makes a contribution to the image?</td>', 'And that right there is probably why your DPL teammates suggested that you submit it for the challenge. For starters, they are more attuned to the public appeal of an image. It''s a good question as to why this image caught my attention. I found that the image really stood out to me among the top 30. (my habit is to view the top 30-50 and bottom 20 of each challenge) I just double checked the top 30 images. This is the only image that really stands out as having an issue where there IS negative space, but that negative space plays no significant role in the image. Yes, it''s the complementary color to the reds in the feathers of the hawk, but how is that doing anything of note in the image. It is simply a background. the impact of the negative space in this image would not change the image significantly by cropping most of it away.  That cannot be said of any other image in the top 30.  I''m not trying to say that I don''t like the image. In fact, I do like the image. that''s why I put the time into the alternate edits to try to give you some options from that really heavily processed look. What I don''t like about the image is that it looks so overprocessed. What I don''t like about it in this challenge is that it is DNMC.What I don''t like about that is not so much that you entered it (we are all entitled to our own takes on the challenge description) but that this is a strong negative effect of the DPL. The DPL has increased the number of ''shoehorn'' entries and this is a very plain demonstration of that. That the DPL is helping you individually is great. Shoot a pic, put it on your DPL forum and learn. Wonderful! I''m all for it. negative space does not = free study.And while we are there, DNMC does not = this is a bad pic. Perhaps you could mention something in the image details about how you feel the Negative Space makes a contribution to the image?', '2007-06-28 22:31:12.000000', NULL, 0),
